@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@attribute [Route(pageUrl + "/{Sample}")]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<SamplePageHeader ChartKey="@SampleChartKeys.PieChart" Sample="@Sample">
    <SiteMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

    <PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
        <SubTitleTemplate>
            @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
        </SubTitleTemplate>
    </PageHero>

    <DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_PieChart" />

    <Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />
</SamplePageHeader>

@* Each SampleSection renders only on its own sample page, /demos/<chart>/<slug>. *@
<CascadingValue Value="@SampleCatalog.Find(SampleChartKeys.PieChart, Sample)">
<SampleSection Slug="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Pie Chart</strong> component visualizes categorical data as proportional slices of a circle, making it ideal for showing parts of a whole.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>PieChart</code> component to your page and set its dimensions (e.g., <code>Width="600"</code>).</li>
                <li>Prepare your chart data by specifying <code>Labels</code> for each category and one or more <code>Datasets</code> with values and background colors.</li>
                <li>Configure chart options such as <code>Responsive</code> and <code>Title</code> using the <code>PieChartOptions</code> object.</li>
                <li>Initialize the chart in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> by calling <code>pieChart.InitializeAsync(chartData, pieChartOptions)</code>.</li>
                <li>Use the demo's interactive buttons to randomize data, add datasets, or add new data points dynamically.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of data preparation, dynamic updates, and chart customization.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        This demo demonstrates how to set up a pie chart, bind your data, and interactively update the chart to fit your application's needs.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="playground">
    <Block>
        Change the main options of a pie chart and watch the chart and its C# update together. The controls cover rotation, circumference, border width and hover offset, plus the legend, the locale and right-to-left layout.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Change a control. The chart is updated with <code>UpdateAsync</code>, so it animates from its current state.</li>
                <li>Pick a <strong>Locale</strong> to see how numbers are formatted for that region, and switch on <strong>Right to left</strong> to mirror the legend and tooltip.</li>
                <li>Copy the C# below the chart. It lists only the settings that differ from their defaults, with the property names the library uses.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <PieChart_Playground />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="data-labels">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Pie Chart</strong> component supports data labels, allowing you to display values or category names directly on each slice of the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Enable data labels by passing the <code>ChartDataLabels</code> plugin when initializing the chart: <code>plugins: new[] { "ChartDataLabels" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Customize the position of data labels for each dataset using the <code>Datalabels.Anchor</code> property (e.g., <code>Anchor.End</code> or <code>Anchor.Center</code>).</li>
                <li>Prepare your chart data and datasets as usual, and bind them to the <code>PieChart</code> component.</li>
                <li>Use the provided buttons to randomize data or add new data points dynamically, and observe how data labels update automatically.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of enabling and customizing data labels in your pie charts.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Data labels make it easier for users to interpret the chart by showing relevant information directly on each pie slice.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_02_Datalabels)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="change-legend-position">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Pie Chart</strong> component allows you to easily change the position of the chart legend to improve readability or match your application's layout.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set the legend position using <code>pieChartOptions.Plugins.Legend.Position</code> (e.g., <code>"top"</code>, <code>"right"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code>, or <code>"left"</code>).</li>
                <li>Update the legend position dynamically by changing this property and calling <code>pieChart.UpdateAsync(chartData, pieChartOptions)</code>.</li>
                <li>Use the demo's buttons to switch the legend position interactively and see the effect in real time.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of configuring and updating the legend position in your pie charts.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Adjusting the legend position helps ensure your chart is clear and fits well within your application's design.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_03_Change_Legend_Position)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a pie slice is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, item index, label, and raw value; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_04_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="show-and-hide-slices">
    <Block>
        Show and hide single slices from C# and read whether a slice is visible.
        A hidden slice stays hidden when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, even if the slices are re-sorted, because slices are matched by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>ToggleDataVisibilityAsync(index)</code>, or <code>HideAsync(0, index)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(0, index)</code>.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>GetDataVisibilityAsync(index)</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync</code>; hidden slices stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_05_Show_Hide_Slices)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. On pie charts the callback receives the slice <code>Index</code>, the legend text and whether the slice is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding slices.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Index</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_06_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("pie-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_07_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different slice. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered slice changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all slices, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a slice is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all slices.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_08_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight slices and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_09_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by <code>Label</code>; datasets that share a label, or have none, pair in order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_10_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="cutout-and-radius">
    <Block>
        <code>Cutout</code> sets the size of the hole in the middle and <code>Radius</code> sets the outer radius. Both take pixels or a percentage string; Chart.js uses a cutout of 0 and a <code>"100%"</code> radius for a pie chart, so any cutout turns the pie into a ring, like a doughnut chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Create <code>PieChartOptions</code> and set <code>Radius = "80%"</code> to shrink the pie inside the available space.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Cutout = "50%"</code> for a hole sized as a percentage of the radius.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Cutout = 40</code> to use a fixed hole of 40 pixels, whatever the chart size.</li>
                <li>Pass the options to <code>InitializeAsync</code> or <code>UpdateAsync</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_11_Cutout_And_Radius)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="half-gauge">
    <Block>
        <code>Rotation</code> sets the start angle of the first arc, in degrees from the top, and <code>Circumference</code> sets how far the arcs sweep. Chart.js uses 0 and 360; a rotation of -90 with a circumference of 180 draws the top half of the pie, and Chart.js scales it to fill the space.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Rotation = -90</code> and <code>Circumference = 180</code> on <code>PieChartOptions</code> for the top half, or <code>Rotation = 90</code> for the bottom half.</li>
                <li>Use other pairs, such as <code>Rotation = -135</code> with <code>Circumference = 270</code>, for a three-quarter pie.</li>
                <li>For a single-value gauge, add two slices (the value and the remainder), give the remainder a grey <code>BackgroundColor</code>, and set <code>Plugins.Legend.Display = false</code>; leave <code>Cutout</code> unset to keep it a solid half pie.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_12_Half_Gauge)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="rotate-and-scale-animation">
    <Block>
        <code>ChartAnimation.AnimateRotate</code> sweeps the arcs in from the start angle, and <code>AnimateScale</code> grows the chart from the center. Chart.js uses <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> for pie charts. Click <strong>Replay</strong> to run the first-render animation again.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Animation = new ChartAnimation { AnimateRotate = false, AnimateScale = true }</code> on <code>PieChartOptions</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Duration</code> on the same <code>ChartAnimation</code> to make the animation longer or shorter.</li>
                <li>To replay, call <code>StopAsync()</code>, then <code>ResetAsync()</code>, then <code>UpdateAsync(chartData, options)</code> on each chart.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_13_Rotate_And_Scale_Animation)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="spacing-mode">
    <Block>
        The dataset <code>Spacing</code> adds a gap between arcs, in pixels, and <code>SpacingMode</code> sets how the gap is measured: <code>"angular"</code> keeps an equal angle (the Chart.js default), <code>"proportional"</code> scales with each arc, and <code>"parallel"</code> keeps the edges of neighbouring arcs parallel.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Spacing = 8</code> on the <code>PieChartDataset</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SpacingMode</code> on the same dataset to <code>"angular"</code>, <code>"proportional"</code> or <code>"parallel"</code>.</li>
                <li>Leave <code>SpacingMode</code> as <code>null</code> to use the Chart.js default.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_14_Spacing_Mode)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="title-and-subtitle">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Title</code> and <code>Plugins.Subtitle</code> draw a title and a smaller line of text, placed by <code>Position</code> and aligned by <code>Align</code>. The subtitle is drawn just after the title on the same side. In this library both default to <code>Color = "black"</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Title = new ChartPluginsTitle { Display = true, Text = "Browser share" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Subtitle = new() { Display = true, Text = "Visitors in October" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Position</code> (<code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code> or <code>"right"</code>) and <code>Align</code> (<code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code> or <code>"end"</code>) to place each one.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding = new() { Top = 10, Bottom = 30 }</code> for more space, and <code>FullSize = false</code> to keep the title within the chart area instead of the full canvas width.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_15_Title_And_Subtitle)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tooltip-options">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Tooltip</code> sets which items the tooltip lists, where it is placed and how it looks. When <code>Mode</code> and <code>Intersect</code> are not set, the tooltip uses the chart <code>Interaction</code> options; with two rings, <code>InteractionMode.Index</code> lists the same slice of both rings.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Tooltip = new ChartPluginsTooltip { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = true }</code>, or use <code>InteractionMode.Nearest</code> with <code>Intersect = false</code> to show the closest slice even between slices.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position</code> to <code>"average"</code> or <code>"nearest"</code>.</li>
                <li>Style the box with <code>Padding</code>, <code>CornerRadius</code>, <code>BorderColor</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code>.</li>
                <li>Style the color boxes with <code>BoxWidth</code>, <code>BoxHeight</code>, <code>BoxPadding</code>, <code>UsePointStyle</code> and <code>MultiKeyBackground</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_16_Tooltip_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="layout-padding">
    <Block>
        <code>Layout</code> adds empty space inside the canvas, around the legend, title and chart. The dashed borders show the canvas edges.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.Padding = 20</code> for the same padding on every side, in pixels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.PaddingSides = new() { Left = 60, Top = 10 }</code> for a value per side; when set, it is sent instead of <code>Padding</code>.</li>
                <li>Leave both unset for no padding.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_17_Layout_Padding)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="aspect-ratio-and-resize-delay">
    <Block>
        With <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code>, the canvas height follows its width by <code>AspectRatio</code> (width / height); Chart.js uses 1 for pie charts. <code>ResizeDelay</code> waits the given milliseconds after the container stops resizing before the chart redraws, which saves work while the window is dragged; resize the window to see the wide chart catch up.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code> (the default) and set <code>AspectRatio</code> to 1, 2 or 3.</li>
                <li>Set <code>ResizeDelay = 300</code> to redraw only after resizing pauses.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DevicePixelRatio = 2</code> to draw the canvas at a higher resolution, for sharper images from <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code> and <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_18_Aspect_Ratio_And_Resize_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-and-elements">
    <Block>
        <code>Elements.Arc</code> sets defaults for every arc, which dataset settings override. <code>Hover</code> sets which slices are highlighted under the pointer, separately from <code>Interaction</code>, which the tooltip keeps using. <code>Events</code> limits the browser events the chart listens to: with <code>Events = ["click"]</code>, moving the pointer does nothing and <code>OnHover</code> does not fire; clicking a slice still highlights it and opens its tooltip, and <code>OnClick</code> fires.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Elements = new ChartElements { Arc = new ChartElementsArc { BorderWidth = 4, BorderAlign = "inner", BorderRadius = 6 } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Arc.Circular = false</code> to draw the arcs with straight edges.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Hover = new ChartHover { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = true }</code> to highlight the same slice in every ring, and give the datasets a <code>HoverOffset</code> to make the highlight visible.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Events = ["click"]</code> to react to clicks only.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_19_Hover_And_Elements)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="colors-plugin">
    <Block>
        The built-in Chart.js colors plugin gives datasets that set no colors a default palette, one color per slice on a pie chart. It does nothing when any dataset sets its own colors, unless <code>ForceOverride = true</code>. Line, radar and scatter datasets send a default color, so on those charts the palette needs <code>ForceOverride = true</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Leave <code>BackgroundColor</code> unset on the <code>PieChartDataset</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new() { Enabled = true }</code>, or leave it unset, to use the palette; <code>Enabled = false</code> leaves the slices in the Chart.js default grey.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new() { ForceOverride = true }</code> to replace the colors the dataset sets.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_20_Colors_Plugin)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="multi-series-pie">
    <Block>
        A pie chart with more than one dataset draws each dataset as its own ring, with the first dataset outermost. It compares the same categories across two series, such as two years.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add one <code>PieChartDataset</code> per ring to <code>ChartData.Datasets</code>. Every dataset uses the same <code>Labels</code>.</li>
                <li>Give the datasets the same <code>BackgroundColor</code> list so a category keeps its colour in every ring.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Weight</code> on a dataset to make its ring thicker or thinner than the others. The default is 1.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(PieChart_Demo_21_Multi_Series_Pie)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>
</CascadingValue>

<SamplePageFooter ChartKey="@SampleChartKeys.PieChart" Sample="@Sample" />

@code {
    /// <summary>The sample slug from the URL, or <see langword="null" /> on the chart overview.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Sample { get; set; }

    private const string pageUrl = DemoRouteConstants.Demos_PieChart;
    private const string pageTitle = "Pie Chart";
    private const string pageDescription = "Explore interactive <code>Blazor Pie Chart</code> examples with source code. Learn how to visualize categorical data, customize chart appearance, and enhance your Blazor applications. Try live demos and discover practical usage scenarios for the Pie Chart component.";
    private const string metaTitle = "Blazor Pie Chart Examples";
    private const string metaDescription = "Explore interactive Blazor Pie Chart examples with source code. Learn how to visualize categorical data, customize chart appearance, and enhance your Blazor applications. Try live demos and discover practical usage scenarios for the Pie Chart component.";
    private const string imageUrl = DemoImageSrcConstants.PieChart;
}
